issues.body:
Found while validating 0.21.0 for link-assistant/hive-mind#3741, with the lightweight `/credentials` entry.

A first pass correctly preserves prose after a vendor token. A second pass masks the already masked value together with that prose. This is a remaining interaction between #132 and #133.

```js
import assert from 'node:assert/strict';
import { createSyncSanitizer } from '@link-foundation/sensitive-data-sanitizer/credentials';
const engine = createSyncSanitizer({ preserveEncoding: true, transformation: { mode: 'hive-mask' } });
const synthetic = ['ghp_', 'Zx9Qw7Er5Ty3Ui1Op0As', '2Df4Gh6Jk8Lz0Xc2Vb4N'].join('');
const once = engine.sanitize(`User token: ${synthetic}. Next sentence`).text;
assert.equal(once, 'User token: ghp…b4N. Next sentence'); // passes
assert.equal(engine.sanitize(once).text, once); // fails: User token: ghp…nce
```

Expected: both passes preserve `. Next sentence` and the original partial mask.

Hive Mind workaround: the verified `exempt` hook keeps a `context` finding that starts with a complete existing mask followed by punctuation/whitespace and prose. Other findings and exact known secrets remain detected. Suggested fix: bound a context finding after an already masked value as well as after a vendor-format value, then recognize the bounded mask as already clean. Please cover repeated plaintext and encoded passes, with another real credential later on the same line.

Version: 0.21.0, Node 26, `@link-foundation/sensitive-data-sanitizer/credentials`.

Two related wrapped-base64 problems remain in credentials-only sanitization:

1. A harmless binary payload wrapped at 76 columns is entirely replaced with a mask once it exceeds 8192 characters. `inspect` calls it `encoded-limit`. A flat JSON image containing the same bytes survives. Finite reproducer:

```js
import { createSyncSanitizer } from '@link-foundation/sensitive-data-sanitizer/credentials';
const s = createSyncSanitizer({ credentialsOnly: true, preserveEncoding: true, transformation: { mode: 'hive-mask' } });
const input = Buffer.alloc(150000, 147).toString('base64').match(/.{1,76}/g).join('\n') + '\n';
console.log(input.length, s.sanitize(input).text.length); // 202632, 8
```

Expected: preserve noncredential binary bytes, keep work bounded. A bounded decoding limit must still fail closed for uninspected text containing credentials; blanket exemption is insufficient.

2. A preceding base64-alphabet word shifts the wrapped candidate's byte alignment. Only the first line is sanitized; the full record can no longer be decoded. Reproducer (synthetic token):

```js
const secret = 'gho_SYNTHETIC0000NotARealToken11111111111';
const json = JSON.stringify({ token: secret, note: 'keep me' });
const wrapped = Buffer.from(json).toString('base64').match(/.{1,20}/g).join('\n');
const input = `start of output\nresponse body=\n${wrapped}\ntrailing line\n`;
const output = s.sanitize(input).text;
console.log(output);
```

Expected: sanitize the complete decoded JSON, preserve `note`, re-encode the complete payload with the original wrapping, and retain surrounding prose. Compare the result with the same JSON without the preceding `body=` line. Hive Mind's existing encoded-credential regression reproduces this difference between whole-text and stream sanitization.

Downstream PR: https://github.com/link-assistant/hive-mind/pull/3741 . We will keep the existing bounded wrapped decoder for these cases, have the library sanitize decoded text, and exempt only encoded findings fully contained in those already-inspected bounded spans. Plaintext findings and exact-known-secret findings remain active. Runs above the local bound retain fail-closed handling.

Library 0.21.0, credentials-only synchronous entry:

```js
import { createSyncSanitizer } from '@link-foundation/sensitive-data-sanitizer/credentials';
const s = createSyncSanitizer({ credentialsOnly: true, transformation: { mode: 'hive-mask' } });
const input = 'const sessionToken = new SessionToken();';
console.log(s.sanitize(input).text);
// const sessionToken = [REDACTED] SessionToken();
```

Expected: preserve the constructor expression. `new` is JavaScript syntax here, not a credential. Other language keyword assignments (await, false, null, undefined, None) already survive. A real credential whose value merely starts with a keyword must still be masked (`token=new-secret-0123456789`).

Downstream regression predates the migration: https://github.com/link-assistant/hive-mind/blob/main/tests/issue-2400-keyword-assignment-not-masked.test.mjs . The library adapter migration in https://github.com/link-assistant/hive-mind/pull/3741 exposed this remaining gap; a narrow `exempt` hook can retain exactly the `new` keyword when followed by a constructor call, while exact known secrets remain unexemptable.

Library 0.21.0 still masks a scheme word via its overlapping Gitleaks generic API-key rule, despite #131's authorization fix:

```js
import { createSyncSanitizer } from '@link-foundation/sensitive-data-sanitizer/credentials';
const s = createSyncSanitizer({ credentialsOnly: true, preserveEncoding: true, transformation: { mode: 'hive-mask' } });
console.log(s.sanitize('Authorization: SharedAccessSignature sr=namespace&sig=abcdefghijklmnopqrstuvwxyz123456&se=123456').text);
// Authorization: Sha…ure sr=…456
```

Expected: `Authorization: SharedAccessSignature sr=…456`. The scheme is structural context, while the opaque payload is still masked. `inspect` reports `gitleaks/generic-api-key` at [15,36), exactly the scheme, and `authorization` plus `context` at [37,96), the payload. This affects the original requirement that **every** Authorization scheme survive, beyond Bearer/Basic/token.

Downstream PR https://github.com/link-assistant/hive-mind/pull/3741 will exempt findings exactly covering a scheme immediately after an Authorization header key and before a separate nonempty payload. It must not exempt a bare credential with no scheme, a payload finding, or an exact known secret.

Library 0.21.0, credentials-only + preserveEncoding + hive-mask, does not preserve an encoded Hive mask when it is short. A first pass re-encodes a 40-character GitHub credential to `Z2hv4oCmOTk5` (base64 for `gho…999`); the next context/Gitleaks pass sees only 12 bytes, below its encoded-candidate threshold, and replaces the mask with `[REDACTED]`.

Minimal reproduction:

```js
import { createSyncSanitizer } from '@link-foundation/sensitive-data-sanitizer/credentials';
const s = createSyncSanitizer({ credentialsOnly: true, preserveEncoding: true, transformation: { mode: 'hive-mask' } });
console.log(s.sanitize('"token":"Z2hv4oCmOTk5"').text);
// "token":"[REDACTED]"
```

Expected: unchanged; it already decodes to a complete sanitized mask. This also breaks the first sanitize call on a base64 credential inside escaped JSON log content, because the library itself repeats verification/rendering. Synthetic end-to-end reproduction is `experiments/issue-3739/encoding-probes.mjs` in https://github.com/link-assistant/hive-mind/pull/3741; existing regression is `tests/test-encoded-secretlint-layer-2156.mjs`.

The downstream workaround will exempt only canonical base64/base64url spellings decoding to a complete existing mask, never arbitrary decodable text or exact known secrets. Please keep the mask contract (first/last three for >12-character credentials) through internal passes, including short re-encodings.

In library 0.21.0, the native `private-key` rule returns `type: 'SECRET'`. Therefore `transformations: { PRIVATE_KEY: { mode: 'redact' } }` is ignored when the general transformation is `hive-mask`; it reveals the first and last three characters of private-key material.

```js
import { createSyncSanitizer } from '@link-foundation/sensitive-data-sanitizer/credentials';
const s = createSyncSanitizer({ credentialsOnly: true, transformation: { mode: 'hive-mask' }, transformations: { PRIVATE_KEY: { mode: 'redact' } } });
const input = '-----BEGIN PRIVATE KEY-----\nSYNTHETIC_PRIVATE_KEY_MATERIAL_123456\n-----END PRIVATE KEY-----';
console.log(s.inspect(input)); // rule: private-key, type: SECRET
console.log(s.sanitize(input).text); // body is SYN…456
```

The downstream policy in https://github.com/link-assistant/hive-mind/pull/3741 requires complete private-key body redaction while keeping PEM markers and surrounding log text. The temporary workaround is a transformation callback selecting native `redact` rendering for private-key rule findings and native `hive-mask` rendering for everything else; no duplicated credential detector or renderer is needed.

Please expose a stable private-key type (including PuTTY keys) or support rule-specific transformations so the intended type override works. Regression: `tests/test-credential-sanitization-2111.mjs` and `experiments/issue-3739/private-key-probe.mjs` downstream.
